# What the build does not need, and mostly must not have.
#
# The Dockerfile copies the whole tree, because restoring one game means restoring the engine
# under it and a project reference is a graph. This is what keeps that honest: without it the
# build context carries every binary already built on this machine, every file ever published
# from it, and the whole git history - which is slow, and worse than slow, because a stale
# `obj/` from a different runtime is exactly the sort of thing that builds and then behaves
# oddly.

.git
.github
.vs
.vscode
.idea

# Built here rather than in the image, and for a different runtime than the image wants.
**/bin
**/obj
publish

# Somebody's own games and somebody's own answers. A record is a game a person played and a
# settings file is how they like to read one; neither belongs to an image, and a house given
# either would come up holding a stranger's evening.
logs
settings.txt
colours.txt
colors.txt

# Read by people, not by the compiler.
**/*.md
LICENSE

# The tools are for a machine with a clone on it. The image builds one project with one line.
tools
tests
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
